        "slide": 7,
        "role": "evidence",
        "subject": "A diagonal limestone bench with three handoff trays. Each has indigo rails, a brass pan holding paper on the clinician's side and an empty pan on the tool's side, now rimmed in true cyan beside a cyan channel lip that leads to a laptop with a solid cyan screen. A short-haired clinician in a white coat sits from behind on a blue stool, and three brass tabs stand at the foot with the third lit.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline, three identical stations hold paper on the person's side and nothing yet on the tool's side. It reads as a handoff not yet made, three times. The accent change makes this clearer. The laptop screen, the three far-pan rims and the channel lip are now one saturated cyan (slide-07-r2c1, slide-07-r2c2, slide-07-r3c2), so the tool's side of every tray reads as belonging to the tool at a glance, and it holds at 432 px. The three rims match each other in intensity. Nothing else moved: the bench, trays, clinician, tabs, pills and type seats are identical to my accepted reading.",
        "weakness": "Minor. Because the cyan skips tone mapping, the rims and the channel lip are flat, unshaded neon lines with no highlight or shading on their bevels (slide-07-r3c2, the tray-three rim; slide-07-r2c1, the station-one rim). They read as graphic outlines on modelled brass rather than lit metal. The laptop screen is a flat cyan field with a hot spot at its base. The earlier secondary notes stand: a translucent patch at the coat's lap and notches in the hairline at the ear.",
        "repair": "None required. At polish, keep the emissive cyan but add a faint view-dependent darkening on the rims' outer bevel so they read as lit material while keeping the measured accent.",
